From 76a87788db915e5837526a943539ae3c5455d56a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 14:42:53 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] dos: patch 0260 -- MPU-401 bytes paced by the CMOS RTC
 periodic interrupt (deferred MIDI kind 3)

0193 replays planned MIDI writes from the SB IRQ, but only for OPL3 and
the blind MPU transport. The WaveBlaster's default transport
(SDL/0097-0098, TIMED-DELAY) needs at least 320 us between two MPU data
bytes, the fix for the DreamBlaster's input overrun. The SB IRQ comes
every ~46 ms, and the games measured put up to 224 bytes into one such
slot. Replaying that in the SB ISR would mean milliseconds of
busy-waiting with interrupts off per IRQ, and a 32-byte-per-IRQ bound
(10 ms) still leaves hundreds of bytes late on 5 Days' first song. So
WaveBlaster stayed on the main-loop poll and played at the render rate.

A faster PIT channel 0 is not an option. uclock() reads it, and ports
pace and measure with uclock() directly. SDL/0122's timebase fixes
only SDL's own counter.

The CMOS RTC's periodic interrupt (IRQ 8) runs from its own 32.768 kHz
crystal and never touches the PIT. Kind 3 uses it as a byte pacer. The
SB ISR releases the queue head when its stamp comes due and sets PIE.
Each RTC tick (2048 Hz, 488 us) writes at most one byte, and only once
the transport's gap has passed in RTC time since the previous one. PIE
goes off when nothing is due. Every ISR here is integer and port I/O
only, with no busy-wait, and code and data are locked.
The compiler does not keep the ISR between its range markers, so the
pump refuses to install unless 0194's whole-program code lock is held.

IRQ 8 is not ours alone. DJGPP's delay(), and so SDL_Delay, is BIOS
INT 15h AH=86h, which the AT BIOS counts down on IRQ 8 through the BIOS
data area. Replacing the vector without honouring that would hang every
SDL_Delay. So the ISR services the contract itself, at the real tick
period read from register A. At expiry it sets count = 0, which is what
DOSBox-X's loop waits for. It also clears the active flag and sets
bit 7 at the user flag, which is what the IBM AT BIOS loop waits for.

Main thread: Enable(3) is accepted only for the TIMED-DELAY direct-port
transport. It sets the RTC to 2048 Hz, PIE off, and clears stale flags.
Enable(0) and device close stop the pump and restore register A, PIE
and the IRQ 8 mask before anything is written directly. What is still
queued, and pause's All Notes Off, go out at the transport's own
spacing with interrupts on. An atexit handler restores the RTC if the
process leaves without closing the device.

The pump writes one byte per tick, so a pause can fall inside a
message. Pause's All Notes Off then leaves the device on another
status, and the rest of the cut message would be read under it (a
note-on's data as a controller change). So the pump tracks the last
status byte it put on the wire. A resume whose queue head is a data
byte re-sends the cut message's status first.

The pump is refused while a BIOS wait is in progress. The killswitch
SDL_HINT_DOS_AUDIO_WB_RTC_PUMP=0 refuses it too, and the port keeps
its poll. SDL_DOSMidiDeferRtcStats reports rate, gap, ticks, serviced
BIOS waits and the longest release-to-write. Default off: nothing
changes unless a port enables kind 3.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Nz9XeKeVG8Ab7dm8esJsMu
